WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.929 --> 00:00:04.170
is the institution left
controls it's own destiny,

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.009
and continues to do all
the things it needs to do

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:08.425
to be sustainable.

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:12.259
So, if you have somebody
who's constantly looking back,

00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:13.922
to draw the past to the future,

00:00:13.922 --> 00:00:15.529
it's not very helpful.
- I respect that.

00:00:15.529 --> 00:00:17.549
- So people have to continually reinvent

00:00:17.549 --> 00:00:19.059
the future going forward.
- I agree.

00:00:19.059 --> 00:00:20.869
- Now, just to show that I am human,

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:21.859
(Gary laughs)

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:25.209
when I left, what I
learned really quickly is

00:00:25.209 --> 00:00:27.307
the projects I got involved with,

00:00:27.307 --> 00:00:31.469
there was a tremendous curiosity

00:00:31.469 --> 00:00:33.219
of transferring my skills,

00:00:33.219 --> 00:00:36.429
having worked in the phone
business for 46 years,

00:00:36.429 --> 00:00:38.249
and transferring them to other things.

00:00:38.249 --> 00:00:41.523
And so, while everything
I've done since I retired

00:00:41.523 --> 00:00:45.663
is neither as important or as big as I did

00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:47.919
when I worked at Verizon.

00:00:47.919 --> 00:00:50.119
Almost everything I do is very important

00:00:50.119 --> 00:00:52.169
to the people I work with.
- No question.

00:00:52.169 --> 00:00:53.699
- So I serve on the board of a hospital,

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:56.312
I serve on the board of
Genome Center in New York.

00:00:56.312 --> 00:00:59.309
I'm working with three or
four startup companies.

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:02.519
I do some consulting with
an investment banking firm.

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:06.259
And all those activities
draw upon my experience.

00:01:06.259 --> 00:01:07.729
- No question.
- And there' a lot of fun.

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:08.959
You have a lot of fun doing that stuff.

00:01:08.959 --> 00:01:10.492
- So let's go way back.

00:01:10.492 --> 00:01:12.302
You're a kid growing up where?

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:17.459
- Well, I'm a Bronxite;
I grew up in the Bronx.

00:01:17.459 --> 00:01:19.199
- [Gary] Were you a big Yankee fan?

00:01:19.199 --> 00:01:20.819
- Well, it's very interesting.

00:01:20.819 --> 00:01:21.652
- Oh, this is gonna be good.
- The question is,

00:01:21.652 --> 00:01:22.975
am I a Yankee fan?

00:01:22.975 --> 00:01:23.808
So sure--
- A man of your generation

00:01:23.808 --> 00:01:26.359
from the Bronx not being
a total diehard Yankee fan

00:01:26.359 --> 00:01:27.192
is gonna have a good story.

00:01:27.192 --> 00:01:28.599
So I'm excited right now.

00:01:28.599 --> 00:01:30.139
- So, I was a Yankee fan.

00:01:30.139 --> 00:01:32.839
And I like the Yankees.
- Keep going.

00:01:32.839 --> 00:01:35.909
- However, in high
school we played baseball

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:37.535
against Ed Kranepool.

00:01:37.535 --> 00:01:39.467
And Kranepool became a Met.

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:41.269
And then the Yankees fired Yogi.

00:01:41.269 --> 00:01:42.169
- [Gary] Yes they did.

00:01:42.169 --> 00:01:44.589
- And once they fired Yogi, I
was no longer a Yankees fan.

00:01:44.589 --> 00:01:46.699
- So you were such a big Yankee fan.

00:01:46.699 --> 00:01:48.437
- Well, how could you not be a Yankee fan

00:01:48.437 --> 00:01:50.243
when you're eight years old.
- No, I apologize.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:51.389
I said the wrong thing.

00:01:51.389 --> 00:01:53.389
You were such a big Yogi fan,

00:01:53.389 --> 00:01:55.829
that the firing of Yogi--
- I hated the way

00:01:55.829 --> 00:01:57.719
the Yankees treated Yogi
when they fired him.

00:01:57.719 --> 00:01:58.552
- [Gary] I get it.

00:01:58.552 --> 00:02:00.519
- And then I needed an excuse
to root for the Mets, anyway.

00:02:00.519 --> 00:02:02.769
So, I've been a Met fan ever since.

00:02:02.769 --> 00:02:03.602
- [Gary] I love it.

00:02:03.602 --> 00:02:06.409
- And I am, today, one of
the small minority owners

00:02:06.409 --> 00:02:07.529
of the Mets.
- Oh okay.

00:02:07.529 --> 00:02:09.517
- So that's even more fun.
- Yeah, that's even more fun.

00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:10.389
You know what's really funny about this,

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:11.909
this is something that,
maybe, people don't know,

00:02:11.909 --> 00:02:13.242
I grew up a big Yankee fan,

00:02:13.242 --> 00:02:15.169
in Jersey, which you have split.

00:02:15.169 --> 00:02:16.229
I was a Jets, Yankee guy.

00:02:16.229 --> 00:02:18.671
So, it was like you had
different kind of allegiances.

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:22.779
But the Yankees won in
'96, and that was it.

00:02:22.779 --> 00:02:25.349
When the Rangers won in '94 to
when the Yankees won in '96,

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:27.879
I stopped rooting for my favorite team.

00:02:27.879 --> 00:02:29.869
Like, literally, just
winning a championship

00:02:29.869 --> 00:02:33.109
has been the finishing
point to the journey.

00:02:33.109 --> 00:02:36.069
And I'm actually raising
my son a Mets fan.

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:37.779
Because his cousins are Met fans.

00:02:37.779 --> 00:02:39.969
And so, we're gonna go
through a very similar thing,

00:02:39.969 --> 00:02:40.869
'cause I'm gonna get sucked in

00:02:40.869 --> 00:02:42.169
at some point.
- I like both teams.

00:02:42.169 --> 00:02:43.599
But I'm a Met fan.

00:02:43.599 --> 00:02:44.432
- [Gary] Yeah, I get it.

00:02:44.432 --> 00:02:45.659
- And when the Yankees play the Mets,

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:46.609
I root for the Mets.

00:02:46.609 --> 00:02:47.609
- [Gary] I understand; you're a Mets fan.

00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:48.442
- Absolutely.

00:02:48.442 --> 00:02:49.275
- What about football? Giants?

00:02:49.275 --> 00:02:50.323
- [Ivan] Jets.

00:02:50.323 --> 00:02:51.219
- Jets!
- Yes.

00:02:51.219 --> 00:02:52.052
- Very good.

00:02:52.052 --> 00:02:54.179
All right, my complete energy has changed.

00:02:54.179 --> 00:02:56.565
Okay, so, go ahead, please.

00:02:56.565 --> 00:02:58.819
- Actually, if you think about it,

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:00.569
growing up in New York in the '60s,

00:03:01.609 --> 00:03:03.289
I wasn't corporate.
- I get it.

00:03:03.289 --> 00:03:04.909
- So I liked the Jets, like the Mets.

00:03:04.909 --> 00:03:06.509
You get that, right.
- I get it.

00:03:06.509 --> 00:03:07.679
What year did you switch to Mets?

00:03:07.679 --> 00:03:10.147
Do you know?
- Well, first year, well.

00:03:10.147 --> 00:03:11.359
- [Gary] Right away.

00:03:11.359 --> 00:03:14.649
The Yankees fired Yogi
in '64 I believe, right?

00:03:14.649 --> 00:03:15.809
And the Mets were in '62.

00:03:15.809 --> 00:03:17.508
So I started right there in '62.

00:03:17.508 --> 00:03:18.469
- And when the Mets made Yogi manager,

00:03:18.469 --> 00:03:19.929
you were pumped.
- I loved it.

00:03:19.929 --> 00:03:21.226
- You must've loved that (laughing).

00:03:21.226 --> 00:03:23.369
- And as a kid I was a Willie Mays fan.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:26.759
- How old were you in '69,
when the Jets and Mets won?

00:03:26.759 --> 00:03:28.109
- I was born in '46.

00:03:28.109 --> 00:03:29.409
- [Gary] Oh, so you were perfect.

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:31.216
You were in a beautiful age.

00:03:31.216 --> 00:03:32.782
So you really got it.

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:38.119
- Oh yeah, no, my
generation, New York sports.

00:03:38.119 --> 00:03:40.019
- Knicks.
- Knicks.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:42.289
Dodgers and Giants were
in New York in the '50s.

00:03:42.289 --> 00:03:43.369
So it was great.

00:03:43.369 --> 00:03:45.108
New York was full of great sports.

00:03:45.108 --> 00:03:45.941
- I love it.

00:03:45.941 --> 00:03:47.719
All right, so, you grow up in the Bronx.

00:03:47.719 --> 00:03:48.809
What kind of kid are you?

00:03:48.809 --> 00:03:50.429
Entrepreneur? Student?

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:52.209
What kind of dude were you?

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:53.049
- [Ivan] Dopey kid.

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:55.629
- Okay, (laughing),
that's the right answer.

00:03:55.629 --> 00:03:57.259
I mean listen, that's the answer.

00:03:57.259 --> 00:03:59.649
- [Ivan] You know, I
lollygagged through school.

00:03:59.649 --> 00:04:03.549
- Did it challenge you?

00:04:03.549 --> 00:04:04.569
- I wouldn't say that.

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:07.889
I think that the schools I
went to were good schools,

00:04:07.889 --> 00:04:08.722
they challenged you.

00:04:08.722 --> 00:04:10.049
But I was a dopey kid.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.419
I didn't pay attention.

00:04:11.419 --> 00:04:14.529
And I ended up going to Lehman College.

00:04:14.529 --> 00:04:16.229
At the time it was Hunter College.

00:04:16.229 --> 00:04:18.503
Went for one year, it's in the book.

00:04:18.503 --> 00:04:22.139
They threw me out after a year;
I didn't have good grades.

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:24.541
And I got a job working
for the phone company,

00:04:24.541 --> 00:04:26.249
luckily.
- As what?

00:04:26.249 --> 00:04:29.429
- As a, well, actually-

00:04:29.429 --> 00:04:31.349
- This is my high school report card.

00:04:31.349 --> 00:04:32.960
No matter how dopey you are,

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.569
you're gonna have to really, really work

00:04:35.569 --> 00:04:37.419
at challenging that beauty.

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:39.915
- I would challenge you with my--

00:04:39.915 --> 00:04:40.883
- Do you have it?

00:04:40.883 --> 00:04:43.129
- I probably have it some place.

00:04:43.129 --> 00:04:44.579
- I need to see that evidence.

00:04:44.579 --> 00:04:45.442
Go ahead.

00:04:45.442 --> 00:04:47.192
- So anyway, I went to college.

00:04:48.129 --> 00:04:49.929
I got a job during the day

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:53.659
working a freight elevator in Manhattan.

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:54.609
Then I got drafted.

00:04:55.599 --> 00:04:57.609
Because I was 1A.

00:04:57.609 --> 00:05:00.989
And lo and behold, I'd
worked for the phone company

00:05:00.989 --> 00:05:03.492
for one month, when I got drafted.

00:05:03.492 --> 00:05:08.459
And I served in the Service for 24 months.

00:05:08.459 --> 00:05:09.839
And the thing about the phone company is,

00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:14.211
they used to write me letters
and send me information.

00:05:14.211 --> 00:05:16.189
They treated me like I
still belonged to them

00:05:16.189 --> 00:05:17.629
even though I only worked
for them for a month.

00:05:17.629 --> 00:05:19.939
- Wow, what company?

00:05:19.939 --> 00:05:21.559
- It was New York Telephone.
- New York Telephone.

00:05:21.559 --> 00:05:22.859
- And then when I came out of the Army,

00:05:22.859 --> 00:05:25.249
I went back to work for
the telephone company,

00:05:25.249 --> 00:05:27.409
and went back to night school,

00:05:27.409 --> 00:05:29.652
and finished night school 12 years later.

00:05:31.509 --> 00:05:33.489
Phone company was always
a great place for me

00:05:33.489 --> 00:05:35.153
because they took care of me

00:05:35.153 --> 00:05:38.129
and they helped me grow up.

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:40.839
So I went from dopey kid to--

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:41.869
- Not as dopey.

00:05:41.869 --> 00:05:42.769
- To an Army Veteran.

00:05:42.769 --> 00:05:44.079
You learn a lot of stuff in the Army.

00:05:44.079 --> 00:05:46.659
And then you work in a big institution,

00:05:46.659 --> 00:05:48.332
you learn a little discipline.

00:05:48.332 --> 00:05:51.289
And then I met my wife when
I got out of the Service.

00:05:51.289 --> 00:05:52.749
And she gave me more discipline.

00:05:52.749 --> 00:05:54.175
(Gary laughs)

00:05:54.175 --> 00:05:55.629
And then you build a career.

00:05:55.629 --> 00:05:57.509
And then, you know, one
thing leads to the other.

00:05:57.509 --> 00:05:58.909
- And let's spend a few minutes

00:05:58.909 --> 00:06:00.569
on one thing leads to the other.

00:06:00.569 --> 00:06:02.570
New York Phone Company until when?

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:03.729
- Sorry?
- You were

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:05.619
in the New York Phone Company until when?

00:06:05.619 --> 00:06:07.529
- Oh no, I was, if you look at it

00:06:07.529 --> 00:06:08.559
the way you asked the question,

00:06:08.559 --> 00:06:13.029
I started in the New York
Telephone Company in 1966

00:06:13.029 --> 00:06:15.622
and was part of the system

00:06:15.622 --> 00:06:17.219
until I retired in 2011.

00:06:17.219 --> 00:06:18.649
- That's what I'm looking for,

00:06:18.649 --> 00:06:20.819
the consolidation, the breaking out,

00:06:20.819 --> 00:06:21.809
the mergers, the end of day,

00:06:21.809 --> 00:06:22.959
how did it all go down?

00:06:23.859 --> 00:06:26.519
- Well, I started in New
York Telephone Company.

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.689
I went to AT&amp;T.

00:06:28.689 --> 00:06:30.789
I worked a little bit at Dell Laboratories

00:06:31.649 --> 00:06:33.859
when AT&amp;T was broken up,

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:35.939
back in the early '80s.

00:06:35.939 --> 00:06:38.589
I went with the New York Regional Company.

00:06:38.589 --> 00:06:41.046
And then they moved me to Washington.

00:06:41.046 --> 00:06:43.049
And I lived in Washington.

00:06:43.049 --> 00:06:46.156
Then we started
consolidating the companies.

00:06:46.156 --> 00:06:47.529
And then we bought cellular companies,

00:06:47.529 --> 00:06:50.649
and we started
reconstructing the industry.

00:06:50.649 --> 00:06:51.869
You know I was one of the people

00:06:51.869 --> 00:06:55.599
that, over the years, had
had a lot of experience

00:06:55.599 --> 00:06:57.289
in working at different
parts of the system.

00:06:57.289 --> 00:07:02.219
So, probably my contribution
to Verizon, probably,

00:07:02.219 --> 00:07:04.999
was to help reconstruct the industry

00:07:04.999 --> 00:07:08.489
after the government broke it up.

00:07:08.489 --> 00:07:12.239
We spent the next 25 years
recreating a different industry,

00:07:12.239 --> 00:07:14.319
but one that had scale and scope,

00:07:14.319 --> 00:07:17.099
focused on wireless, focused on broadband.

00:07:17.099 --> 00:07:18.069
All the things you guys like.

00:07:18.069 --> 00:07:20.760
- Can we focus a little
bit on the breakup?

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:21.729
- [Ivan] Yeah, sure.

00:07:21.729 --> 00:07:25.729
- How long was that in the air
before it actually went down?

00:07:25.729 --> 00:07:27.599
How long was it in the
public consciousness,

00:07:27.599 --> 00:07:29.454
or behind the scenes, or in the press,

00:07:29.454 --> 00:07:31.949
with that, hey, this thing is actually

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:32.799
gonna get broken up?
- So, good question.

00:07:32.799 --> 00:07:36.089
So it's a good question
because it has relevance

00:07:36.089 --> 00:07:37.099
to what's going on today

00:07:37.099 --> 00:07:38.752
with the tech companies.
- That's why I'm asking.

00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:41.000
By the way, in that crappy report card,

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.259
the only good grades I had were History.

00:07:43.259 --> 00:07:45.109
Besides Gym; I was a Gym Class hero.

00:07:45.109 --> 00:07:46.839
Four A's; they're the only
A's on my report card,

00:07:46.839 --> 00:07:49.039
but I asked that question,

00:07:49.039 --> 00:07:51.689
and you're very astute, it's self-serving,

00:07:51.689 --> 00:07:55.059
I do think history is an
enormous indicator to the future.

00:07:55.059 --> 00:07:58.109
And I haven't had the
opportunity to really dig in.

00:07:58.109 --> 00:08:00.059
So I'm taking my at-bat right now.

00:08:00.059 --> 00:08:00.892
I'm curious.

00:08:00.892 --> 00:08:05.219
- So, in a nutshell, if you
looked at the old Bell system,

00:08:05.219 --> 00:08:07.789
the old AT&amp;T in the '70s,

00:08:07.789 --> 00:08:12.399
there were two complaints
that just wouldn't go away.

00:08:12.399 --> 00:08:14.839
The first is they were
stifling new entrants

00:08:14.839 --> 00:08:16.529
from coming into the market.

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:18.329
So, they were a big monopoly.

00:08:18.329 --> 00:08:19.789
And no matter how many companies

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:21.859
wanted to get into the phone business,

00:08:21.859 --> 00:08:25.138
even thought the technology
was there for it to happen,

00:08:25.138 --> 00:08:28.649
it never really blossomed.

00:08:28.649 --> 00:08:31.379
And the second thing is
the government itself

00:08:31.379 --> 00:08:35.689
kinda gave up and said,
they're too big to regulate.

00:08:35.689 --> 00:08:37.419
We can't figure it out.

00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:40.519
And so, to your question,
that was going on

00:08:40.519 --> 00:08:43.960
for probably five to 10 years before that.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.219
But it never got traction.

00:08:46.219 --> 00:08:47.052
But then it started

00:08:47.052 --> 00:08:48.709
to get traction.
- Why?

00:08:48.709 --> 00:08:50.289
From your one man's point of view?

00:08:50.289 --> 00:08:53.229
- Well, okay, A, the government

00:08:53.229 --> 00:08:55.079
was getting more serious about it.

00:08:55.079 --> 00:08:57.939
And they were suing the
company all the time.

00:08:57.939 --> 00:09:00.257
They were getting extremely aggressive

00:09:00.257 --> 00:09:02.899
about trying to correct those things

00:09:02.899 --> 00:09:04.059
that they thought were a problem,

00:09:04.059 --> 00:09:07.559
which were barriers to entry
and they couldn't regulate it.

00:09:07.559 --> 00:09:11.252
But the other thing that
happened is, the company itself

00:09:11.252 --> 00:09:13.772
lost the ability to grow.

00:09:14.759 --> 00:09:17.289
So, once AT&amp;t itself said, look,

00:09:17.289 --> 00:09:20.149
I can't deal with all this
government interference

00:09:20.149 --> 00:09:21.061
and regulation,

00:09:21.061 --> 00:09:22.993
and I'm having trouble growing.

00:09:22.993 --> 00:09:24.971
- Myself.
- Myself.

00:09:24.971 --> 00:09:26.729
That's when they got together

00:09:26.729 --> 00:09:28.239
and they agreed on a consent decree

00:09:28.239 --> 00:09:29.689
that broke up the company.

00:09:29.689 --> 00:09:31.002
So, if you look at today,

00:09:32.101 --> 00:09:34.639
some of the tech
companies, not all of them,

00:09:34.639 --> 00:09:35.629
but some of them,

00:09:35.629 --> 00:09:37.539
they look too big to regulate.

00:09:37.539 --> 00:09:39.345
They've become global.

00:09:39.345 --> 00:09:44.345
They've had no allegiance,
nor grounding, in any country.

00:09:44.469 --> 00:09:46.948
They operate to a
different set of standards.

00:09:46.948 --> 00:09:50.879
And people are getting
frustrated that simple things

00:09:50.879 --> 00:09:54.159
that should be governable, aren't.

00:09:54.159 --> 00:09:56.439
Now, they haven't run into
the problem with new growth.

00:09:56.439 --> 00:09:58.819
- That's right.
- But they will.

00:09:58.819 --> 00:10:00.646
At some point, growth will slow,

00:10:00.646 --> 00:10:03.779
and there will be forces

00:10:03.779 --> 00:10:07.469
in which governments around
the world and companies

00:10:07.469 --> 00:10:09.849
will come to the conclusion
there's another paradigm

00:10:09.849 --> 00:10:11.829
that's necessary.
- I understand.

00:10:11.829 --> 00:10:13.639
- So, these things run in cycles.

00:10:13.639 --> 00:10:16.356
Now, I tend to be more market-based.

00:10:16.356 --> 00:10:20.049
So I'm not looking for
government mandated solutions.

00:10:20.049 --> 00:10:22.869
I think tech companies
deserve a lot of credit

00:10:22.869 --> 00:10:24.084
for what they've built.

00:10:24.084 --> 00:10:27.449
But they have run into a situation,

00:10:27.449 --> 00:10:28.926
which not all of them
are serving customers

00:10:28.926 --> 00:10:30.239
the way they should.

00:10:30.239 --> 00:10:31.072
- I understand.

00:10:31.072 --> 00:10:33.289
- And so, therefor, I think
these things will come to a head

00:10:33.289 --> 00:10:35.429
over the course of the
next four or five years.

00:10:35.429 --> 00:10:36.429
- One of the things
that's interesting to me

00:10:36.429 --> 00:10:37.729
in the current state of tech companies

00:10:37.729 --> 00:10:40.199
versus the AT&amp;T parallel

00:10:40.199 --> 00:10:42.772
is there weren't alternatives

00:10:42.772 --> 00:10:45.172
to use a different phone provider.

00:10:46.629 --> 00:10:48.479
The alternatives were super difficult.

00:10:48.479 --> 00:10:52.651
Your ability to not use Facebook
or Google is quite high.

00:10:52.651 --> 00:10:57.651
My ability to use Yahoo or
Bing as a search engine,

00:10:57.999 --> 00:11:01.289
or not be on Facebook or Instagram,

00:11:01.289 --> 00:11:03.319
is extraordinarily high.

00:11:03.319 --> 00:11:04.779
I think one of the most fascinating things

00:11:04.779 --> 00:11:06.309
that are happening right now,

00:11:06.309 --> 00:11:08.108
in the current conversation
of tech companies,

00:11:08.108 --> 00:11:12.569
is the shocking inability for Americans

00:11:12.569 --> 00:11:15.592
to take any level of
accountability on themselves.

00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:19.429
- Well, so this is interesting.

00:11:19.429 --> 00:11:22.962
If you go to, history would say,

00:11:24.599 --> 00:11:28.169
people used to make that
argument about Coca Cola.

00:11:28.169 --> 00:11:30.338
They're the cause of obesity.

00:11:30.338 --> 00:11:33.549
Well, people would make that
case against the phone company

00:11:33.549 --> 00:11:36.609
by saying we're teaching
kids to sit on the phone.

00:11:36.609 --> 00:11:39.579
- Say that one more time,
because I'm really excited

00:11:39.579 --> 00:11:40.412
at what you just said.

00:11:40.412 --> 00:11:42.139
I wanna get back to Coke.
- I'm not advocating.

00:11:42.139 --> 00:11:42.972
- No, I know you're not.

00:11:42.972 --> 00:11:44.599
- But as a businessperson.
- No, no.

00:11:44.599 --> 00:11:46.189
I love what you just said.

00:11:46.189 --> 00:11:47.909
The amount of parents right now

00:11:47.909 --> 00:11:51.959
that are too young to remember an era

00:11:51.959 --> 00:11:53.453
when the phone company was blamed

00:11:53.453 --> 00:11:55.792
for kids being on the phone all day.

00:11:56.779 --> 00:11:58.219
I mean they're just doing the same thing.

00:11:58.219 --> 00:12:01.109
I watch parents, yet,
complain about the iPad.

00:12:01.109 --> 00:12:05.129
And I'm like, take the
iPad away from your kid.

00:12:05.129 --> 00:12:09.292
As if the iPad, as if
Apple, controls them.

00:12:10.379 --> 00:12:13.519
- No, but as a business.
- Yeah, I'm listening.

00:12:13.519 --> 00:12:14.774
- We have to be sensitive to

00:12:14.774 --> 00:12:16.798
these kinds of arguments.
- Of course.

00:12:16.798 --> 00:12:17.939
Of course.

00:12:17.939 --> 00:12:19.479
- We have to balance making money

00:12:19.479 --> 00:12:21.829
against doing the right thing.
- Of course.

00:12:21.829 --> 00:12:23.969
- But yet you have to pay
attention to these things

00:12:23.969 --> 00:12:24.802
over the course

00:12:24.802 --> 00:12:25.769
of your history.
- Of course.

00:12:25.769 --> 00:12:27.019
- Because the public will
hold you accountable.

00:12:27.019 --> 00:12:27.852
- Of course.

00:12:27.852 --> 00:12:31.013
But, where's the public's accountability?

00:12:31.013 --> 00:12:32.839
I mean, to me, that is the great,

00:12:32.839 --> 00:12:34.089
fascinating thing about this.

00:12:34.089 --> 00:12:36.009
You know, maybe I'm hypersensitive to this

00:12:36.009 --> 00:12:38.062
because I was born in the Soviet Union.

00:12:39.209 --> 00:12:44.204
But, listen, I commend Apple debating

00:12:44.204 --> 00:12:47.079
should they make apps that
stop you from screen time.

00:12:47.079 --> 00:12:48.899
It's all very wonderful.

00:12:48.899 --> 00:12:50.389
Coca Cola has Diet Coke.

00:12:50.389 --> 00:12:52.482
There is zero-sugar Coca Cola.

00:12:53.932 --> 00:12:55.449
Where does it stop and start?

00:12:55.449 --> 00:12:57.829
Mike Bloomberg, in this great city,

00:12:57.829 --> 00:13:00.609
tried to limit how big a soda is,

00:13:00.609 --> 00:13:03.219
and got the reaction in the other way.

00:13:03.219 --> 00:13:06.491
I think what's fascinating
is watching people,

00:13:06.491 --> 00:13:11.491
I'm fascinated by 70 or
80 years of prosperity.

00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:14.309
Listen, you're a man from
a different generation

00:13:14.309 --> 00:13:16.959
than a lot of the people that
I'm referring to right now,

00:13:16.959 --> 00:13:20.329
who, you know, I believe that
we've had so much prosperity

00:13:20.329 --> 00:13:21.819
in this country,

00:13:21.819 --> 00:13:23.879
that we've gotten quite,

00:13:23.879 --> 00:13:27.669
I'm just fascinated by
lack of accountability.

00:13:27.669 --> 00:13:28.549
I really am.

00:13:28.549 --> 00:13:31.129
- Well, so this is a political discussion.

00:13:31.129 --> 00:13:31.962
So I'm not gonna

00:13:31.962 --> 00:13:32.795
go into it.
- Yeah, and honestly,

00:13:32.795 --> 00:13:33.628
you'll appreciate this,

00:13:33.628 --> 00:13:35.179
I am not, actually, to your point,

00:13:35.179 --> 00:13:36.832
I'm not really interested
in the political.

00:13:36.832 --> 00:13:38.120
- But as a business--
- Please.

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:38.953
- As a business discussion.
- To me,

00:13:38.953 --> 00:13:40.039
it's a business discussion.

00:13:40.039 --> 00:13:43.144
- Yeah, so I agree with
almost everything you said.

00:13:43.144 --> 00:13:45.839
But there is an issue
that we have looked at

00:13:45.839 --> 00:13:47.629
the last 30 years.

00:13:47.629 --> 00:13:50.869
And we have created more inequality

00:13:50.869 --> 00:13:52.852
than we intended to create.

00:13:52.852 --> 00:13:55.119
So we have overdosed on technology,

00:13:55.119 --> 00:13:56.689
we've overdosed on--
- Yeah, but listen.

00:13:56.689 --> 00:13:58.709
- Displacing people with technology.

00:13:58.709 --> 00:13:59.542
- You're preaching.

00:13:59.542 --> 00:14:01.029
- [Ivan] So we have to figure that out.

00:14:01.029 --> 00:14:01.862
- I'm with you.

00:14:01.862 --> 00:14:02.695
And, to your point,

00:14:02.695 --> 00:14:03.989
not to make this a political conversation,

00:14:03.989 --> 00:14:05.439
here's the problem:

00:14:05.439 --> 00:14:08.859
Our overdosing on technology
hasn't even begun.

00:14:08.859 --> 00:14:09.999
There's a naivete,

00:14:09.999 --> 00:14:11.459
right?
- Exactly.

00:14:11.459 --> 00:14:12.292
- I mean tech,

00:14:12.292 --> 00:14:13.959
this is the, right this second,

00:14:13.959 --> 00:14:16.489
is the least amount of
technology involvement

00:14:16.489 --> 00:14:18.289
in perpetuity.
- Correct.

00:14:18.289 --> 00:14:20.069
- So, we need to accept that.

00:14:20.069 --> 00:14:22.442
Because ideology is super fun.

00:14:22.442 --> 00:14:26.739
But making your kids spend
an hour less on the iPad

00:14:26.739 --> 00:14:29.839
is not gonna solve a lot
of these human truths.

00:14:29.839 --> 00:14:32.199
- So, if we look at,
I'll use our business.

00:14:32.199 --> 00:14:34.009
- And I apologize, real
quick, to cutting you off.

00:14:34.009 --> 00:14:35.419
I think, to segue a little bit,

00:14:35.419 --> 00:14:37.569
'cause I think it's gonna
bring value to the audience,

00:14:37.569 --> 00:14:39.784
what I love about the
conversation we're having,

00:14:39.784 --> 00:14:44.166
is these are the macro-trends that CEO,

00:14:46.799 --> 00:14:48.509
where she and he has to be thinking about

00:14:48.509 --> 00:14:50.008
on an everyday basis of the micro,

00:14:50.008 --> 00:14:52.919
these things we're talking
about in the world,

00:14:52.919 --> 00:14:54.819
is how you thought about the four walls,

00:14:54.819 --> 00:14:57.979
the virtual four walls
of Verizon for 16 years.

00:14:57.979 --> 00:15:00.329
Everything is important at all times.

00:15:00.329 --> 00:15:02.059
The amount of things that happen here.

00:15:02.059 --> 00:15:04.867
The point you were making about Coca Cola.

00:15:04.867 --> 00:15:05.939
- [Ivan] Yeah, I wasn't picking on them.

00:15:05.939 --> 00:15:07.849
- No you weren't, you were
actually doing exactly right.

00:15:07.849 --> 00:15:10.669
I spend every day about
perception becomes reality

00:15:10.669 --> 00:15:12.419
becomes what you have to deal with.

00:15:13.799 --> 00:15:15.390
- So if I can bring this,

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:17.052
fast-forward?
- Please.

00:15:17.899 --> 00:15:20.169
- So one of the things our industry

00:15:20.169 --> 00:15:21.957
always had a lot of pride in.

00:15:21.957 --> 00:15:25.219
When we moved from first
generation wireless to third,

00:15:25.219 --> 00:15:27.452
now to fifth generation wireless, 5G,

00:15:28.779 --> 00:15:32.209
what we hope will happen
is that industries

00:15:32.209 --> 00:15:35.819
that have been untouched by technology,

00:15:35.819 --> 00:15:38.049
will be disrupted and transformed.

00:15:38.049 --> 00:15:39.194
- Well, especially 5G.

00:15:39.194 --> 00:15:40.879
I do not believe--
- So, let's take an example.

00:15:40.879 --> 00:15:43.372
- People do not understand
the technology jump

00:15:43.372 --> 00:15:44.292
that this is about to be.

00:15:44.292 --> 00:15:45.125
- So let me use

00:15:45.125 --> 00:15:46.879
my favorite example.
- Please.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:51.109
- Banking has been changed, right?

00:15:51.109 --> 00:15:53.639
Retail has been changed.

00:15:53.639 --> 00:15:55.849
If you go into a classroom,
it looks the same

00:15:55.849 --> 00:15:58.099
as it did 40 years ago.

00:15:58.099 --> 00:16:01.619
And so, when I look at education,

00:16:01.619 --> 00:16:03.759
where is the technological disruption

00:16:03.759 --> 00:16:04.592
that should have occurred in that

00:16:04.592 --> 00:16:06.329
over the last 30, 40 years?

00:16:06.329 --> 00:16:08.209
So we didn't see that happen.

00:16:08.209 --> 00:16:09.809
Healthcare's another place.

00:16:09.809 --> 00:16:11.639
The environment's a third.

00:16:11.639 --> 00:16:15.399
So, to me the business
community has lots to give.

00:16:15.399 --> 00:16:18.289
But there's an uneven focus

00:16:18.289 --> 00:16:20.289
around where technology gets deployed.

00:16:20.289 --> 00:16:21.569
Some of that is inertia.

00:16:21.569 --> 00:16:22.549
Some of that is government.

00:16:22.549 --> 00:16:26.819
Some of that is entrenched interests.

00:16:26.819 --> 00:16:30.779
But the fact is we need to take
the technological revolution

00:16:30.779 --> 00:16:34.349
and apply it aggressively across industry.

00:16:34.349 --> 00:16:35.449
So we need,

00:16:35.449 --> 00:16:37.929
you know Amazon did what it did.

00:16:37.929 --> 00:16:40.482
We need an Amazon of education.

00:16:40.482 --> 00:16:43.859
We need and Amazon of environment

00:16:43.859 --> 00:16:45.969
to really apply the technology.

00:16:45.969 --> 00:16:48.889
And that would create jobs,
and it would create value,

00:16:48.889 --> 00:16:52.619
and it would start to slant
what we're now looking at

00:16:52.619 --> 00:16:55.799
is all this inequality of
jobs and educational skills.

00:16:55.799 --> 00:16:57.159
- I understand.

00:16:57.159 --> 00:16:59.579
Let's take this in a different turn.

00:16:59.579 --> 00:17:02.979
What about, in your career,

00:17:02.979 --> 00:17:04.909
who were your biggest mentors

00:17:04.909 --> 00:17:06.649
and what were some of
the best pieces of advice

00:17:06.649 --> 00:17:07.609
you got from them?

00:17:10.989 --> 00:17:13.672
- Early in my career, I
was probably uncoachable.

00:17:14.649 --> 00:17:16.397
- [Gary] 'Cause you thought you
were right about everything?

00:17:16.397 --> 00:17:17.809
- No, because, you know I'm a city kid.

00:17:17.809 --> 00:17:19.571
You figure you can figure
everything out yourself.

00:17:19.571 --> 00:17:22.549
But what I think, the way I would

00:17:22.549 --> 00:17:23.979
answer the question.
- You can fully tell,

00:17:23.979 --> 00:17:24.812
I believe it.

00:17:24.812 --> 00:17:25.959
- Yeah, no, I can see that.

00:17:25.959 --> 00:17:26.809
I can see that.

00:17:26.809 --> 00:17:29.933
What I did though, is I
used to copy the people

00:17:29.933 --> 00:17:32.122
who did things I liked.

00:17:32.969 --> 00:17:36.689
And so, somebody, I used to
like the way they dressed.

00:17:36.689 --> 00:17:39.049
Well somebody else, in a meeting,

00:17:39.049 --> 00:17:42.209
would handle bad news very well.

00:17:42.209 --> 00:17:44.589
Or somebody would have
a positive attitude.

00:17:44.589 --> 00:17:46.032
And so I used to use,

00:17:46.032 --> 00:17:49.907
my mentors were those
people who had attributes

00:17:49.907 --> 00:17:51.419
that I thought were pretty cool.

00:17:51.419 --> 00:17:54.496
- You know, I actually
analyzed that as a street kid.

00:17:54.496 --> 00:17:57.314
You're using your environment
to make adjustments.

00:17:57.314 --> 00:17:58.499
- That's true.

00:17:58.499 --> 00:18:01.189
And I learned, as I
moved up in the company,

00:18:01.189 --> 00:18:02.599
people watched everything I did.

00:18:02.599 --> 00:18:03.779
- [Gary] 100%

00:18:03.779 --> 00:18:08.779
- So, to me, imitation is
the best form of teaching

00:18:08.989 --> 00:18:10.659
that you can give people
if you do the right thing

00:18:10.659 --> 00:18:11.492
or if you try to do the right thing.

00:18:11.492 --> 00:18:13.529
- And it's an incredible
sense of responsibility

00:18:13.529 --> 00:18:15.759
once you make that realization.

00:18:15.759 --> 00:18:17.209
- So, to answer your question,

00:18:17.209 --> 00:18:20.450
my mentors were the people
who handled controversy well,

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:24.969
who had a good attitude,
who were always prepared.

00:18:24.969 --> 00:18:26.979
- How much do you think
positivity matters?

00:18:26.979 --> 00:18:27.839
I mean that.

00:18:27.839 --> 00:18:28.909
Practical positivity.

00:18:28.909 --> 00:18:30.039
When I say something like that,

00:18:30.039 --> 00:18:32.559
how does that register in your mind?

00:18:32.559 --> 00:18:34.435
- So, remember now, I'm an East Coast kid.

00:18:34.435 --> 00:18:37.189
So when I hear that term,

00:18:37.189 --> 00:18:40.239
I wanna make sure I ferret out the fluff.

00:18:40.239 --> 00:18:41.072
- [Gary] Go ahead.

00:18:41.072 --> 00:18:43.859
- So, I like people with
a positive attitude.

00:18:43.859 --> 00:18:47.154
But I like people who are
practical, street smart,

00:18:47.154 --> 00:18:49.239
and get things done.

00:18:49.239 --> 00:18:52.499
Not people who like to
yak it to 20,000 feet,

00:18:52.499 --> 00:18:53.332
just get--
- You mean,

00:18:53.332 --> 00:18:54.412
pontificating positivity?

00:18:54.412 --> 00:18:55.654
- [Ivan] That's a word.

00:18:55.654 --> 00:18:58.639
- That's like, you notice
how I framed it up, right?

00:18:58.639 --> 00:19:00.761
I think positivity is imperative,

00:19:00.761 --> 00:19:04.769
but if it lacks practicality,
it's an ideology.

00:19:04.769 --> 00:19:06.453
- Right, so--
- And it's vulnerable.

00:19:06.453 --> 00:19:10.039
- So there was one person
who once gave me some advice,

00:19:10.039 --> 00:19:11.529
that's close to this point, Gary,

00:19:11.529 --> 00:19:14.519
and that is, we were in a meeting,

00:19:14.519 --> 00:19:17.819
and a very contentious subject.

00:19:17.819 --> 00:19:21.659
And as typical in a
meeting on the East Coast.

00:19:21.659 --> 00:19:23.299
When people get upset with each other,

00:19:23.299 --> 00:19:24.999
they attack each other personally.

00:19:25.969 --> 00:19:29.719
And so, my boss, who was from Michigan,

00:19:29.719 --> 00:19:32.489
on the way back to our office,

00:19:32.489 --> 00:19:35.112
he took me aside and he said, Ivan,

00:19:35.112 --> 00:19:37.479
why do people around
here attack each other

00:19:37.479 --> 00:19:38.479
and not the problem?

00:19:39.499 --> 00:19:41.449
And he was sending me a message:

00:19:41.449 --> 00:19:42.729
Don't do that.

00:19:42.729 --> 00:19:43.562
I never forgot.

00:19:43.562 --> 00:19:45.609
Because here I am, 40 years later,

00:19:45.609 --> 00:19:46.829
and I've never forgotten.

00:19:46.829 --> 00:19:48.798
And I sit in a room and
I can tell the people

00:19:48.798 --> 00:19:53.229
who get frustrated with
the facts, or the issue,

00:19:53.229 --> 00:19:54.959
or can't articulate their point of view.

00:19:54.959 --> 00:19:57.259
And the first thing they
do is attack somebody

00:19:57.259 --> 00:19:58.849
instead of attacking the problem.

00:19:58.849 --> 00:19:59.682
- [Gary] I like that, Ivan.

00:19:59.682 --> 00:20:00.515
- And it's just something,

00:20:00.515 --> 00:20:01.359
so that's an example.

00:20:01.359 --> 00:20:02.192
- I'm a buyer of that.

00:20:02.192 --> 00:20:04.359
- So that's positivity.
- I'm an East Coast Kid.

00:20:04.359 --> 00:20:06.569
But I always say that I'm unemotional.

00:20:06.569 --> 00:20:09.539
And what I'm really saying
is what you just said.

00:20:09.539 --> 00:20:12.201
Which is, the biggest thing
I preach to my direct reports

00:20:12.201 --> 00:20:14.749
is if you're not capable
of being the bigger person

00:20:14.749 --> 00:20:16.249
in every situation,

00:20:16.249 --> 00:20:18.439
then you're vulnerable with me because,

00:20:18.439 --> 00:20:20.205
to your Michigan boss' point,

00:20:20.205 --> 00:20:24.589
it's just there's no
value in that behavior.

00:20:24.589 --> 00:20:25.699
And once in a while,

00:20:25.699 --> 00:20:27.469
you know, I love when you
have such a good rapport

00:20:27.469 --> 00:20:28.439
when you can do it

00:20:28.439 --> 00:20:30.829
and it's almost like you're competing

00:20:30.829 --> 00:20:32.129
on the basketball court.

00:20:32.129 --> 00:20:35.079
But to your point, it's so, that's cool.

00:20:35.079 --> 00:20:35.912
I love that.

00:20:35.912 --> 00:20:37.317
That's a great story.
- If I can bring us back

00:20:37.317 --> 00:20:38.150
to the book.
- You can do

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:38.983
anything you want, of course.

00:20:38.983 --> 00:20:39.816
- If I could bring us back to the book.

00:20:39.816 --> 00:20:41.769
There are lots of chapters in there,

00:20:41.769 --> 00:20:43.195
but there's a couple favorite chapters

00:20:43.195 --> 00:20:44.359
that happened.
- Please.

00:20:44.359 --> 00:20:47.189
- One of them is the
story of how our company

00:20:48.429 --> 00:20:50.154
was able to deliver after 9/11.

00:20:50.154 --> 00:20:52.459
- Let's talk about it.
- So, very quickly.

00:20:52.459 --> 00:20:54.629
9/11 occurred.
- Right here.

00:20:55.674 --> 00:20:59.099
- We were one of the first
people that were called

00:20:59.099 --> 00:21:01.089
by the Department of Homeland Security

00:21:01.089 --> 00:21:03.509
because, they said, you guys
must know what's going on.

00:21:03.509 --> 00:21:04.342
What's going on?

00:21:04.342 --> 00:21:06.259
And we said, we don't
know what's going on.

00:21:06.259 --> 00:21:09.796
We saw the second plane hit the tower.

00:21:09.796 --> 00:21:12.149
Within 10 minutes.
- When you say you saw?

00:21:12.149 --> 00:21:14.619
- We were sitting in our office
at the 42nd Street building,

00:21:14.619 --> 00:21:16.049
looking down.

00:21:16.049 --> 00:21:17.669
We saw the first tower on flames.

00:21:17.669 --> 00:21:19.269
And we saw the plane
hit the second building.

00:21:19.269 --> 00:21:21.019
- You physically saw,
from your office window?

00:21:21.019 --> 00:21:22.779
- About 30 of us.

00:21:22.779 --> 00:21:23.949
- I'm listening.

00:21:23.949 --> 00:21:27.736
- Our chief engineer said, right there.

00:21:27.736 --> 00:21:28.569
- [Gary] (fingers snap) On that second.

00:21:28.569 --> 00:21:30.569
- He said, they don't put that fire out,

00:21:30.569 --> 00:21:31.921
that building's gonna collapse.

00:21:31.921 --> 00:21:32.754
- And you guys were like,

00:21:32.754 --> 00:21:34.141
what the hell are you talking about?

00:21:34.141 --> 00:21:37.199
- And we told other people that.

00:21:37.199 --> 00:21:38.349
We didn't know if that was right.

00:21:38.349 --> 00:21:39.523
But he was right.

00:21:39.523 --> 00:21:42.029
And we said to him,
Larry, why is that right?

00:21:42.029 --> 00:21:44.359
And he said, because all
the steel's gonna melt.

00:21:44.359 --> 00:21:46.582
The center of the building
is gonna collapse.

00:21:47.799 --> 00:21:52.026
So, our job then, 'cause
all of Wall Street was out.

00:21:52.026 --> 00:21:55.109
All of Manhattan was out.

00:21:55.109 --> 00:21:59.862
And what happened after
that was, the next morning,

00:22:01.189 --> 00:22:04.721
still flames, still trying
to look for survivors.

00:22:04.721 --> 00:22:06.929
The calls were coming in
from all over the country

00:22:06.929 --> 00:22:08.009
about when is the Stock Exchange

00:22:08.009 --> 00:22:09.959
gonna be up and running?

00:22:09.959 --> 00:22:13.174
And every single one of the
trading partners at Wall Street

00:22:13.174 --> 00:22:16.649
said, yeah, we'll be up Thursday.

00:22:16.649 --> 00:22:18.289
But we knew that wasn't true.

00:22:18.289 --> 00:22:19.139
- [Gary] Why did you
know that to not be true?

00:22:19.139 --> 00:22:21.996
- Because they couldn't get
their people into the buildings.

00:22:21.996 --> 00:22:23.089
Right?

00:22:23.089 --> 00:22:25.989
They didn't have alternative locations.

00:22:25.989 --> 00:22:27.839
So, I remember Dick Grasso,

00:22:27.839 --> 00:22:30.639
who was the Chairman of the
Stock Exchange at the time,

00:22:30.639 --> 00:22:32.609
he called a meeting of
everybody in the city.

00:22:32.609 --> 00:22:33.479
And he brought our people in.

00:22:33.479 --> 00:22:37.279
And he said, what are we gonna do?

00:22:37.279 --> 00:22:42.279
And our guys just sat there
and said, there's no way

00:22:43.299 --> 00:22:44.364
that you're gonna be able to,

00:22:44.364 --> 00:22:45.749
(phone ringing) sorry.

00:22:45.749 --> 00:22:46.582
- [Gary] No problem.

00:22:46.582 --> 00:22:47.769
- Get the Stock Exchange up and running

00:22:47.769 --> 00:22:49.089
because people won't have facilities,

00:22:49.089 --> 00:22:50.739
in empty sites.

00:22:50.739 --> 00:22:52.119
They weren't even letting people

00:22:52.119 --> 00:22:53.944
into Ground Zero at the time.

00:22:53.944 --> 00:22:56.299
And anybody who wanted to set
up facilities in New Jersey,

00:22:56.299 --> 00:22:58.222
or someplace else, needed time to do it.

00:22:58.222 --> 00:23:03.222
So, what was interesting is
our people basically said,

00:23:03.909 --> 00:23:05.089
we could do the following,

00:23:05.089 --> 00:23:07.389
but here's all the things
you will have to do.

00:23:08.359 --> 00:23:11.019
And it was very interesting
how Grasso just gravitated

00:23:11.019 --> 00:23:13.519
to our chief guy, Larry Babbio,

00:23:13.519 --> 00:23:15.579
and said, so what do you need to do?

00:23:15.579 --> 00:23:19.009
And so we talked about what
everybody needed to do.

00:23:19.009 --> 00:23:21.249
And the story here was great

00:23:21.249 --> 00:23:23.699
because we were running
cables outta buildings.

00:23:23.699 --> 00:23:27.159
We were doing all sorts of
things that we needed to do

00:23:27.159 --> 00:23:31.289
but we were also helping
other people do their jobs.

00:23:31.289 --> 00:23:35.342
So this is the lesson on
9/11 for us: accountability.

00:23:36.839 --> 00:23:38.879
There is no such thing as accountability

00:23:38.879 --> 00:23:41.632
if you do your job and
the next guy can't do his.

00:23:42.629 --> 00:23:44.992
Accountability means we all win together.

00:23:45.929 --> 00:23:49.899
And so we ended up committing
to getting the Stock Exchange

00:23:49.899 --> 00:23:52.089
up and running the following Monday.

00:23:52.089 --> 00:23:55.449
And in the process of doing
that, we developed relationships

00:23:55.449 --> 00:23:58.129
with the top 60 or 70 trading partners,

00:23:58.129 --> 00:23:59.409
the Goldman Sachs, the Merrill Lynches,

00:23:59.409 --> 00:24:00.659
all those companies,

00:24:00.659 --> 00:24:02.855
to help them make sure they
could open their facilities,

00:24:02.855 --> 00:24:05.197
they had enough circuits
to get things done,

00:24:05.197 --> 00:24:07.379
they had the generators and the power,

00:24:07.379 --> 00:24:09.719
and everything else that needed to occur

00:24:09.719 --> 00:24:11.089
to get the country up and running.

00:24:11.089 --> 00:24:14.723
And it was one of the greatest
experiences of our careers,

00:24:14.723 --> 00:24:16.699
'cause it was a big deal.
- For sure.

00:24:16.699 --> 00:24:18.127
- Was such a big deal.

00:24:18.127 --> 00:24:21.642
But it taught out company, back in 2001,

00:24:23.349 --> 00:24:25.229
what real accountability is.

00:24:25.229 --> 00:24:27.159
Real accountability is
not just doing your job,

00:24:27.159 --> 00:24:30.229
but doing your job and helping
the people you work with

00:24:30.229 --> 00:24:31.069
do their jobs.

00:24:31.069 --> 00:24:32.479
- [Gary] I understand.

00:24:32.479 --> 00:24:34.989
Ivan, for, I have such a large audience

00:24:34.989 --> 00:24:36.758
of 20, 30, 40 year old entrepreneurs,

00:24:36.758 --> 00:24:41.109
or number twos and threes
in smaller companies

00:24:41.109 --> 00:24:43.489
quite a bit because the
nature of my business,

00:24:43.489 --> 00:24:46.199
senior executives in Fortune 500s.

00:24:46.199 --> 00:24:47.469
What are some of the,

00:24:47.469 --> 00:24:49.669
just back to the context
of this conversation,

00:24:49.669 --> 00:24:51.410
what are some of the things,

00:24:51.410 --> 00:24:54.579
back to those 20 or 30 people
that you spent time with,

00:24:54.579 --> 00:24:58.839
startups, boards, I'm
sure family and friends,

00:24:58.839 --> 00:25:01.479
what are some of the
tried-est and truest things

00:25:01.479 --> 00:25:04.289
that come to mind that bring value.

00:25:04.289 --> 00:25:06.879
What is some of the advice, or thoughts,

00:25:06.879 --> 00:25:09.948
that you've seen bring
actual value to others

00:25:09.948 --> 00:25:13.444
in their careers or ambitions
that you feel are like,

00:25:13.444 --> 00:25:15.469
I don't wanna call them
your Bill of Rights

00:25:15.469 --> 00:25:16.302
or your commandments,

00:25:16.302 --> 00:25:17.579
but yes, that kind of stuff.

00:25:17.579 --> 00:25:20.769
What are some of the truest
things that you believe in

00:25:20.769 --> 00:25:23.162
as an operator, as an executive?

00:25:24.629 --> 00:25:27.239
- Probably the thing that
comes to mind first, Gary,

00:25:27.239 --> 00:25:31.129
is curiosity and the
willingness to ask questions.

00:25:31.129 --> 00:25:32.499
- Having the humility to know

00:25:32.499 --> 00:25:34.662
that you don't know everything.

00:25:34.662 --> 00:25:36.679
- To help people think through things.

00:25:36.679 --> 00:25:38.339
- Yeah, I mean, do you think it's hard,

00:25:38.339 --> 00:25:41.339
when you get to such a senior
level as you've sat on,

00:25:41.339 --> 00:25:43.339
to show that?

00:25:43.339 --> 00:25:45.819
Was it hard for you to develop the ability

00:25:45.819 --> 00:25:50.819
to manifest curiosity into
what is humility of a leader?

00:25:50.819 --> 00:25:53.062
Or was that something
that came natural to you?

00:25:53.969 --> 00:25:57.082
- No, I think in my case,

00:25:58.848 --> 00:26:03.289
I always came with the attitude
that I came from the Bronx.

00:26:03.289 --> 00:26:07.939
So I don't think it ever dawned
on me that I had the right

00:26:07.939 --> 00:26:10.004
to act like I graduated MIT.

00:26:10.004 --> 00:26:10.837
- [Gary] I understand.

00:26:10.837 --> 00:26:12.229
- I just didn't do that.

00:26:12.229 --> 00:26:15.109
So I always worried
about making a mistake.

00:26:15.109 --> 00:26:15.942
- [Gary] That's right.

00:26:15.942 --> 00:26:18.749
- So, in my case, I think
asking a lot of questions,

00:26:18.749 --> 00:26:21.589
being prepared, taking the next step.

00:26:21.589 --> 00:26:23.509
- You brought up being
prepared a couple times.

00:26:23.509 --> 00:26:26.667
It's not something I think a lot about.

00:26:26.667 --> 00:26:28.519
I think I stay in my lane,

00:26:28.519 --> 00:26:31.109
so thus, by macro, I'm prepared.

00:26:31.109 --> 00:26:32.510
You've brought it up three times now.

00:26:32.510 --> 00:26:35.209
Talk to me about that for five seconds.

00:26:35.209 --> 00:26:37.219
Are you a--
- I don't think I've ever seen

00:26:37.219 --> 00:26:38.849
a presentation brought to me

00:26:39.929 --> 00:26:42.446
that there weren't three good questions

00:26:42.446 --> 00:26:44.022
that you couldn't ask.

00:26:45.159 --> 00:26:48.169
Not to catch people,
but just to dig deeper,

00:26:48.169 --> 00:26:51.519
understand the secondary and
the tertiary effects of things.

00:26:51.519 --> 00:26:54.935
So, I think it's really
important to be prepared.

00:26:54.935 --> 00:26:57.609
I'll just give you one
anecdote that's in the book.

00:26:57.609 --> 00:26:58.442
- [Gary] Please.

00:26:58.442 --> 00:27:00.079
- It probably helped me make my career

00:27:00.079 --> 00:27:01.939
even though it was a freak thing.

00:27:01.939 --> 00:27:02.772
- [Gary] I'm excited already.

00:27:02.772 --> 00:27:07.229
Seriously, I love this stuff.
- It was in 1981,

00:27:07.229 --> 00:27:11.299
and somehow I was on some
sort of a task force.

00:27:11.299 --> 00:27:13.139
And I was supposed to make a presentation

00:27:13.139 --> 00:27:15.089
to all of the high-level people at AT&amp;T,

00:27:15.089 --> 00:27:17.039
including Charlie Brown.

00:27:17.039 --> 00:27:18.926
Charlie Brown was the Chairman
and CEO of the company.

00:27:18.926 --> 00:27:20.962
And I was sort of a mid-level person.

00:27:22.529 --> 00:27:23.362
- [Gary] Go ahead.

00:27:23.362 --> 00:27:24.457
- And Charlie Brown was a legendary guy.

00:27:24.457 --> 00:27:25.439
- [Gary] Yeah, big time.

00:27:25.439 --> 00:27:28.079
- So he shows up--
- Had you interacted

00:27:28.079 --> 00:27:29.209
with Charlie, at this point?
- Never.

00:27:29.209 --> 00:27:30.889
Never met him before.
- So are you scared crapless?

00:27:30.889 --> 00:27:32.829
Or you felt prepared?

00:27:32.829 --> 00:27:34.279
- I was prepared.
- Okay, keep going.

00:27:34.279 --> 00:27:35.899
- And I'll tell you why I was prepared.

00:27:35.899 --> 00:27:37.196
- [Gary] I can't wait, I know
there's a punchline coming.

00:27:37.196 --> 00:27:39.329
- It had to be 50 people in the room,

00:27:39.329 --> 00:27:40.443
all these vice presidents.

00:27:40.443 --> 00:27:42.579
And the meeting was eight
o'clock in the morning.

00:27:42.579 --> 00:27:44.857
I got there at 6:30 a.m. to
make sure everything worked.

00:27:44.857 --> 00:27:47.435
And he comes--
- What was the technology

00:27:47.435 --> 00:27:49.999
of the day, in '81, working?

00:27:49.999 --> 00:27:52.966
- It was overhead graphs.

00:27:52.966 --> 00:27:55.119
You had to make them.

00:27:55.119 --> 00:27:57.659
And so he walks in at
one minute to 8:00 a.m.

00:27:57.659 --> 00:28:01.399
and he sits down, no chit
chat, he says, proceed.

00:28:01.399 --> 00:28:02.549
Dead silence.

00:28:02.549 --> 00:28:04.599
And everybody's sitting
there with their arms folded.

00:28:04.599 --> 00:28:05.589
Nobody's smiling anymore.

00:28:05.589 --> 00:28:08.439
- [Gary] Not the warmest room of all time?

00:28:08.439 --> 00:28:09.299
- No, it was okay.

00:28:09.299 --> 00:28:10.769
So, I started the presentation,

00:28:10.769 --> 00:28:12.172
and he goes, I know that.

00:28:13.179 --> 00:28:14.709
Okay, put up the second chart.

00:28:14.709 --> 00:28:16.279
He goes, I know that.

00:28:16.279 --> 00:28:17.336
Third chart, he goes I know that.

00:28:17.336 --> 00:28:19.349
And I said no you don't.

00:28:19.349 --> 00:28:20.182
- Really?

00:28:20.182 --> 00:28:21.239
- Yeah, and I didn't mean it

00:28:21.239 --> 00:28:23.199
to be disrespectful.
- It just came natural.

00:28:23.199 --> 00:28:25.449
- Right, I just said, I
know you don't know this.

00:28:25.449 --> 00:28:27.549
And he, dead silence in the room,

00:28:27.549 --> 00:28:30.929
he looks at me and he goes, okay, proceed.

00:28:30.929 --> 00:28:32.542
So, he showed me something.

00:28:34.049 --> 00:28:35.789
He didn't get upset.

00:28:35.789 --> 00:28:38.779
He just tested it, and
then we talked it through.

00:28:38.779 --> 00:28:40.589
Now the gist of the whole presentation

00:28:40.589 --> 00:28:43.709
was, even though AT&amp;T
signed this consent decree

00:28:43.709 --> 00:28:45.309
with the government,

00:28:45.309 --> 00:28:47.719
and they were gonna break up the system.

00:28:47.719 --> 00:28:50.609
My point to them was the
government wasn't gonna cooperate

00:28:50.609 --> 00:28:52.809
with making this happen easily.

00:28:52.809 --> 00:28:55.539
So we were gonna have to
do some things on our own.

00:28:55.539 --> 00:28:58.829
And I remember his lawyers
and all his vice presidents

00:28:58.829 --> 00:29:00.492
would go nuts.

00:29:00.492 --> 00:29:02.039
And he just sat there.

00:29:02.039 --> 00:29:02.872
He just sat there.

00:29:02.872 --> 00:29:04.019
And at the end of the
presentation he goes,

00:29:04.019 --> 00:29:04.852
thank you very much.

00:29:04.852 --> 00:29:06.489
He got up and left.

00:29:06.489 --> 00:29:07.579
That's what he did.

00:29:07.579 --> 00:29:10.699
And, of course, everybody
came up to me and said,

00:29:10.699 --> 00:29:13.686
you're screwed; you
might as well quit now.

00:29:13.686 --> 00:29:16.959
And I remember nothing
happened for about a week.

00:29:16.959 --> 00:29:19.049
Then I got a call from my vice president,

00:29:19.049 --> 00:29:21.639
who was a very big, big person.

00:29:21.639 --> 00:29:24.439
He says, come into the office.

00:29:24.439 --> 00:29:26.939
And he brought in all the naysayers.

00:29:26.939 --> 00:29:30.096
And he said, now before
everybody starts screaming,

00:29:30.096 --> 00:29:33.868
he goes, and I had a beard
at the time, full beard.

00:29:33.868 --> 00:29:34.869
(Gary laughing)

00:29:34.869 --> 00:29:37.829
Full beard; nobody had a beard at AT&amp;T.

00:29:37.829 --> 00:29:39.079
Walter Kelly was his name,

00:29:39.079 --> 00:29:41.449
and he said to everybody in the room,

00:29:41.449 --> 00:29:45.669
he said, we have four sons,
and they all have facial hair.

00:29:45.669 --> 00:29:47.579
This was in 1980, right.

00:29:47.579 --> 00:29:49.619
And he goes, we can't get them to shave.

00:29:49.619 --> 00:29:51.919
He goes, what is it with you young people?

00:29:51.919 --> 00:29:53.143
- You were 45 though,

00:29:53.143 --> 00:29:55.439
right?
- No, I was almost,

00:29:55.439 --> 00:29:56.436
um 1980?

00:29:56.436 --> 00:29:57.779
- 'Cause you were born in '46, you said?

00:29:57.779 --> 00:29:58.612
- '66.

00:29:58.612 --> 00:29:59.663
- Oh, '66, right.

00:29:59.663 --> 00:30:01.457
- '66, no, no, 46.
- Oh so you're,

00:30:01.457 --> 00:30:02.504
no, no, 46.
- I was born in '46.

00:30:02.504 --> 00:30:04.344
- So you're 44, 45 actually.

00:30:04.344 --> 00:30:06.129
- Well no--
- Right, this is '81,

00:30:06.129 --> 00:30:08.249
you said.
- '81, yeah, right, right.

00:30:08.249 --> 00:30:09.269
Let's do the math, right?

00:30:09.269 --> 00:30:14.257
'86 I was 40, right?
- Oh, I'm sorry, you're right.

00:30:14.257 --> 00:30:15.832
My math was off, go ahead.

00:30:15.832 --> 00:30:18.029
Keep going.
- Do I have the math right?

00:30:18.029 --> 00:30:19.109
- Yeah, you got the math right.

00:30:19.109 --> 00:30:20.059
I got it wrong.

00:30:20.059 --> 00:30:20.892
Go ahead.

00:30:20.892 --> 00:30:21.725
- I wouldn't want my shareholders

00:30:21.725 --> 00:30:22.649
to think I couldn't--
- No, no, my math is off.

00:30:22.649 --> 00:30:23.482
I apologize.

00:30:23.482 --> 00:30:24.379
Go ahead.
- So, anyway, he said,

00:30:24.379 --> 00:30:26.319
he said, what is it with you young people?

00:30:26.319 --> 00:30:27.319
And we just laughed.

00:30:27.319 --> 00:30:29.439
And then he said to everybody in the room,

00:30:29.439 --> 00:30:30.779
listen to the young man.

00:30:30.779 --> 00:30:32.329
That's just what he said.

00:30:32.329 --> 00:30:34.499
Now this was a high-level person at AT&amp;T.

00:30:34.499 --> 00:30:36.709
You would never think he would have

00:30:36.709 --> 00:30:40.179
that kind of flexibility
and humility, but he did.

00:30:40.179 --> 00:30:41.179
So it's a lesson--
- Winning player.

00:30:41.179 --> 00:30:43.309
- A lesson I never forgot.

00:30:43.309 --> 00:30:44.619
- I believe in it the most.

00:30:44.619 --> 00:30:47.299
- Never forgot.
- Nothing makes me happier

00:30:47.299 --> 00:30:48.252
than being wrong.

00:30:49.091 --> 00:30:50.029
I always think I'm right.

00:30:50.029 --> 00:30:51.446
- But, Gary, if I wasn't prepared

00:30:51.446 --> 00:30:52.899
and didn't have the self-confidence.

00:30:52.899 --> 00:30:53.909
- [Gary] You would have never said it.

00:30:53.909 --> 00:30:54.742
- I would have never said it.

00:30:54.742 --> 00:30:55.575
- [Gary] I totally get it, brother.

00:30:55.575 --> 00:30:56.478
- I would have just moved

00:30:56.478 --> 00:30:57.379
through the conversation.
- My man, my man,

00:30:57.379 --> 00:31:00.699
honestly, as somebody who always thinks

00:31:00.699 --> 00:31:02.919
there's a second game in the room,

00:31:02.919 --> 00:31:04.989
this is basically how I live my life.

00:31:04.989 --> 00:31:09.179
The amount I know about
executives that work for me,

00:31:09.179 --> 00:31:12.389
clients, co-founders,
fancy people in the world

00:31:12.389 --> 00:31:14.019
that people think are brilliant,

00:31:14.019 --> 00:31:16.969
the amount of things I know
by asking a singular question

00:31:16.969 --> 00:31:18.039
at a meeting.

00:31:18.039 --> 00:31:20.929
The amount of times I've made up something

00:31:20.929 --> 00:31:23.694
to see if that person just
agrees to appease the sit.

00:31:23.694 --> 00:31:25.869
It's the most important,
you have to get it.

00:31:25.869 --> 00:31:27.759
When you are sitting in a world

00:31:27.759 --> 00:31:30.339
where you walk in one
minute before a meeting

00:31:30.339 --> 00:31:32.429
and you've gotta navigate that much,

00:31:32.429 --> 00:31:34.184
and time is your biggest enemy.

00:31:34.184 --> 00:31:39.184
You have to create all
these secondary skill sets

00:31:39.509 --> 00:31:41.929
to be able to understand
the situation at hand.

00:31:41.929 --> 00:31:44.389
I've an incredible read on humans,

00:31:44.389 --> 00:31:45.834
based on moments like that.

00:31:45.834 --> 00:31:48.489
- Coming back to Verizon Untethered,

00:31:48.489 --> 00:31:52.909
we believed, as a culture,
we were as curious

00:31:52.909 --> 00:31:54.422
as any culture could ever be.

00:31:55.289 --> 00:31:58.619
So, the head of my wireless company,

00:31:58.619 --> 00:32:00.409
or the head of some of the units,

00:32:00.409 --> 00:32:02.549
when they knew I was
traveling around the country

00:32:02.549 --> 00:32:04.015
for whatever reason,

00:32:04.015 --> 00:32:07.329
they would give me a list
of stores to go into,

00:32:07.329 --> 00:32:09.002
and say go make a field visit.

00:32:10.009 --> 00:32:11.039
And I used to do that.

00:32:11.039 --> 00:32:12.439
And we all used to do that.

00:32:12.439 --> 00:32:13.307
And we would just go--
- Did the stores

00:32:13.307 --> 00:32:14.919
know you were coming?

00:32:14.919 --> 00:32:15.842
- [Ivan] No.

00:32:15.842 --> 00:32:17.209
- Because one of the
things I hated growing up

00:32:17.209 --> 00:32:19.409
in the liquor business is,

00:32:19.409 --> 00:32:21.079
my dad had a big liquor store,

00:32:21.079 --> 00:32:23.219
and when the Smirnoffs, or the Absolut,

00:32:23.219 --> 00:32:25.489
or the Budweiser big dogs would come,

00:32:25.489 --> 00:32:27.129
we would stage the store.

00:32:27.129 --> 00:32:28.846
And I remember when I finally was 16

00:32:28.846 --> 00:32:30.569
and had some level of a voice,

00:32:30.569 --> 00:32:32.559
which I really didn't, even at 16,

00:32:32.559 --> 00:32:34.129
but I'd been working in the
store for a couple years

00:32:34.129 --> 00:32:35.219
at that point.

00:32:35.219 --> 00:32:38.669
I said, Dad, we're not
doing that person a service.

00:32:38.669 --> 00:32:40.519
And he looked at me like I was crazy.

00:32:41.399 --> 00:32:44.259
I remember when I took
over operations at 22,

00:32:44.259 --> 00:32:46.259
I never staged the store.

00:32:46.259 --> 00:32:47.958
And the distributor would be pissed.

00:32:47.958 --> 00:32:50.559
The regional managers,
the holding companies

00:32:50.559 --> 00:32:51.619
would be pissed.

00:32:51.619 --> 00:32:54.739
And the CEO, eight out of
10 times, was thrilled.

00:32:54.739 --> 00:32:56.569
It was the only truth they got.

00:32:56.569 --> 00:32:58.423
I know nobody's drinking
your Vodka, it sucks.

00:32:58.423 --> 00:33:00.929
- There's an anecdote in here

00:33:00.929 --> 00:33:02.769
that my successor, Lowell McAdam,

00:33:02.769 --> 00:33:04.959
used to also make field visits.

00:33:04.959 --> 00:33:06.589
He'd go into a store.

00:33:06.589 --> 00:33:07.719
If there was a light bulb out,

00:33:07.719 --> 00:33:08.979
he would unscrew the light bulb out

00:33:08.979 --> 00:33:11.626
and he would mail it to
the district manager.

00:33:11.626 --> 00:33:12.756
- [Gary] I get it.

00:33:12.756 --> 00:33:15.168
- And you do that across the company

00:33:15.168 --> 00:33:18.269
consistently for three, four, five years,

00:33:18.269 --> 00:33:19.219
people pay attention

00:33:19.219 --> 00:33:20.559
to details.
- 100,000%

00:33:20.559 --> 00:33:22.689
- And it's a big thing.

00:33:22.689 --> 00:33:24.219
So, curiosity's one thing.

00:33:24.219 --> 00:33:25.258
The other thing is--

00:33:25.258 --> 00:33:26.091
- Let's get a phone call in here.

00:33:26.091 --> 00:33:27.821
- Yeah, the other thing is operational

00:33:27.821 --> 00:33:29.293
excellence.
- Go ahead.

00:33:29.293 --> 00:33:31.889
- We were sticklers for that.

00:33:31.889 --> 00:33:33.156
Now you're always trying to figure out

00:33:33.156 --> 00:33:35.016
if you're doing the right
thing for your customers,

00:33:35.016 --> 00:33:38.369
but we really worked hard at that.

00:33:38.369 --> 00:33:39.669
- You guys built a crazy company.

00:33:39.669 --> 00:33:40.549
- [Ivan] We did.

00:33:40.549 --> 00:33:41.382
- You really did.

00:33:41.382 --> 00:33:42.648
- [Ivan] A lot of people did it.

00:33:42.648 --> 00:33:46.072
- Who were your core
competitors when you guys,

00:33:47.309 --> 00:33:48.609
in your prime of your career,

00:33:48.609 --> 00:33:49.632
when you kind of took on.

00:33:49.632 --> 00:33:50.829
What was the comp--
- Well, AT&amp;T, and Sprint,

00:33:50.829 --> 00:33:52.873
and T-Mobile were there.

00:33:52.873 --> 00:33:54.359
And later on,

00:33:54.359 --> 00:33:56.244
on the broadband side.
- Who's disappeared?

00:33:56.244 --> 00:33:57.733
- [Phone AI] Enjoy this
Verizon Ringback tone

00:33:57.733 --> 00:33:58.566
while your party

00:33:58.566 --> 00:33:59.539
is being reached.
- Who was a competitor

00:33:59.539 --> 00:34:02.352
when you firs started that
is no longer out there.

00:34:02.352 --> 00:34:03.539
Anybody stand out?
- Hello?

00:34:03.539 --> 00:34:04.609
- Oh, we got somebody, I apologize.

00:34:04.609 --> 00:34:05.749
Who's this?

00:34:05.749 --> 00:34:07.079
Robert, it's Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:34:07.079 --> 00:34:07.912
You're on with Ivan.

00:34:07.912 --> 00:34:09.149
Please say hello.

00:34:09.149 --> 00:34:10.020
- [Robert] Hey Gary V.

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:11.589
Awesome, you guys are amazing.

00:34:11.589 --> 00:34:12.439
Love your show.

00:34:12.439 --> 00:34:13.299
- Thank you.

00:34:13.299 --> 00:34:14.750
What's your question?

00:34:14.750 --> 00:34:18.019
- [Robert] Basically I
actually am a current member

00:34:18.019 --> 00:34:19.079
of Verizon Wireless,

00:34:19.079 --> 00:34:21.179
and the whole thing with 5G

00:34:21.179 --> 00:34:23.343
and our stock in telecommunications,

00:34:23.343 --> 00:34:25.069
what's our future gonna look like?

00:34:25.069 --> 00:34:26.655
Or, even for small businesses,

00:34:26.655 --> 00:34:29.247
is that gonna make everything grow

00:34:29.247 --> 00:34:31.071
with small business and
5G it's gonna bring?

00:34:31.071 --> 00:34:32.369
In your opinion.

00:34:32.369 --> 00:34:35.109
- Ivan, as somebody who's
just kind of headline reading

00:34:35.109 --> 00:34:36.569
at this point, and started digging in,

00:34:36.569 --> 00:34:39.419
but I've dug in 'cause
there are some clear things

00:34:39.419 --> 00:34:40.504
that I'm interested in with 5G.

00:34:40.504 --> 00:34:41.991
Maybe, based on that question,

00:34:41.991 --> 00:34:43.779
for everybody who's listening,

00:34:43.779 --> 00:34:44.612
and I don't know how much

00:34:44.612 --> 00:34:46.409
or how little you've gone deep into 5G.

00:34:46.409 --> 00:34:49.029
But, if you have gone a little bit deeper,

00:34:49.029 --> 00:34:50.139
which I'm gonna make assumptions,

00:34:50.139 --> 00:34:52.989
but not necessarily based on
where you are at this point.

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:56.259
What are some of the real bullet points

00:34:56.259 --> 00:34:58.469
that you think people are either missing

00:34:58.469 --> 00:34:59.391
or the most exciting things to you

00:34:59.391 --> 00:35:01.009
about this technology?

00:35:01.009 --> 00:35:02.943
- So let me make sure I qualify by saying

00:35:02.943 --> 00:35:04.729
that I no longer speak for the company.

00:35:04.729 --> 00:35:05.588
- [Gary] I know that.

00:35:05.588 --> 00:35:06.421
(Robert laughs)
- But I love the company.

00:35:06.421 --> 00:35:07.254
- [Gary] Understood.

00:35:07.254 --> 00:35:09.669
- And thank you for your
call and Verizon Wireless.

00:35:09.669 --> 00:35:11.223
You guys are doing great, so keep it up.

00:35:11.223 --> 00:35:12.869
- [Robert] Oh, thank you very much.

00:35:12.869 --> 00:35:16.232
- So 5G, what's different about 5G?

00:35:16.232 --> 00:35:17.529
There's a couple of things.

00:35:17.529 --> 00:35:19.779
So first of all, the
number of the devices.

00:35:19.779 --> 00:35:21.869
- [Gary] Rob, thanks for calling brother.

00:35:21.869 --> 00:35:24.891
- The number of devices you
can connect in one square mile

00:35:24.891 --> 00:35:28.119
is an order of magnitude
higher than it is today.

00:35:28.119 --> 00:35:29.609
So you could create services

00:35:29.609 --> 00:35:31.209
in which you have millions of devices

00:35:31.209 --> 00:35:33.889
in a very small geographic area.

00:35:33.889 --> 00:35:35.639
They use less power.

00:35:35.639 --> 00:35:38.469
So you can do the same
thing with far less power.

00:35:38.469 --> 00:35:41.699
The latency, the speed of
switching calls, much lower.

00:35:41.699 --> 00:35:42.619
- [Gary] That seems to be

00:35:42.619 --> 00:35:43.729
a really exciting part.
- That's a huge issue.

00:35:43.729 --> 00:35:44.562
- The latency thing seems to be

00:35:44.562 --> 00:35:46.989
a big play.
- And then the amount of data

00:35:46.989 --> 00:35:50.532
that you can transmit is an
order of magnitude tenfold.

00:35:51.609 --> 00:35:54.229
The other thing is, the
cost to transmit the data

00:35:54.229 --> 00:35:55.329
is much lower.

00:35:55.329 --> 00:35:58.469
So when you look at--
- I think the name itself,

00:35:58.469 --> 00:35:59.562
confuses the end consumer,

00:35:59.562 --> 00:36:01.489
'cause it's one tick up,

00:36:01.489 --> 00:36:03.849
yet the magnitude is so extraordinary.

00:36:03.849 --> 00:36:05.249
- Yeah, and that's the same thing happened

00:36:05.249 --> 00:36:06.749
when we went from three to 4G.
- That's right.

00:36:06.749 --> 00:36:09.659
But, three to 4G, and
correct me if I'm wrong,

00:36:09.659 --> 00:36:11.719
it seems, especially on latency,

00:36:11.719 --> 00:36:13.629
which I think effects a lot of things,

00:36:13.629 --> 00:36:17.673
it seems like the jump here
is kind of a signature jump.

00:36:17.673 --> 00:36:21.689
I just think a lot of people
assume stuff's gonna be faster.

00:36:21.689 --> 00:36:23.369
And there's just so much more to it.

00:36:23.369 --> 00:36:24.689
- So let's use examples.

00:36:24.689 --> 00:36:27.609
I mean, you've heard
these examples before.

00:36:27.609 --> 00:36:30.619
We'll take the easy
one: self-driving cars.

00:36:30.619 --> 00:36:32.169
- This is a big one for me.

00:36:32.169 --> 00:36:34.589
- You know whether you
believe you like it or not,

00:36:34.589 --> 00:36:38.629
the fact is, over time, when
you can reduce the latency

00:36:38.629 --> 00:36:42.405
and increase the topography and the maps,

00:36:42.405 --> 00:36:44.429
you eliminate a lot of problems.

00:36:44.429 --> 00:36:45.709
And you could actually run cities.

00:36:45.709 --> 00:36:48.659
So, Verizon today is
introducing smart-cities

00:36:48.659 --> 00:36:50.639
in Sacramento and Portland.

00:36:50.639 --> 00:36:52.379
And traffic lights that can do things.

00:36:52.379 --> 00:36:53.629
That's one thing.

00:36:53.629 --> 00:36:55.413
So, something more exotic.

00:36:55.413 --> 00:36:57.285
Think of healthcare.

00:36:57.285 --> 00:37:01.725
With broadband, you could do an operation

00:37:01.725 --> 00:37:06.616
with a patient sitting
in Auburn University

00:37:06.616 --> 00:37:09.409
and the doctor could be in San Francisco.

00:37:09.409 --> 00:37:11.069
- Because of the lack of latency, right?

00:37:11.069 --> 00:37:13.256
- Because of the speed of the connection.

00:37:13.256 --> 00:37:14.089
- It's in real time,

00:37:14.089 --> 00:37:14.925
in essence.
- And the clarity,

00:37:14.925 --> 00:37:15.999
in real time.

00:37:15.999 --> 00:37:19.109
And just think of what you
could do for, for example,

00:37:19.109 --> 00:37:20.659
health services to rural America.

00:37:20.659 --> 00:37:22.469
Or health services into poor areas,

00:37:22.469 --> 00:37:23.989
and the people who can't travel.

00:37:23.989 --> 00:37:24.822
That kind of thing.

00:37:24.822 --> 00:37:26.649
Just think what kind of services

00:37:26.649 --> 00:37:29.073
you can offer to the
elderly, the latchkey.

00:37:29.073 --> 00:37:32.749
That's not our thing,
in the phone company.

00:37:32.749 --> 00:37:34.101
I mean we do the infrastructure.

00:37:34.101 --> 00:37:34.934
- [Gary] That's right, you're the pipes.

00:37:34.934 --> 00:37:35.839
- We put the network in.

00:37:35.839 --> 00:37:39.045
And I always get uncomfortable
that we have to explain

00:37:39.045 --> 00:37:41.169
what the applications are,

00:37:41.169 --> 00:37:42.139
when you've got--
- Right, 'cause the market

00:37:42.139 --> 00:37:43.109
needs to take over.
- Yeah, the market

00:37:43.109 --> 00:37:44.809
will catch up to it and it'll do it.

00:37:44.809 --> 00:37:45.642
The market'll create it.

00:37:45.642 --> 00:37:48.679
- But the infrastructure
network and pipes, in essence,

00:37:48.679 --> 00:37:50.901
allow for so many more innovations.

00:37:50.901 --> 00:37:54.359
- Well, just think,
what you're reading now,

00:37:54.359 --> 00:37:57.589
which, again, you could like it or not,

00:37:57.589 --> 00:37:59.529
but all of a sudden the gambling industry

00:37:59.529 --> 00:38:00.957
has figured this out.

00:38:00.957 --> 00:38:03.357
And just think of what
they're gonna do with--

00:38:03.357 --> 00:38:06.239
- You could literally bet on this pitch,

00:38:06.239 --> 00:38:08.157
because the speed in
which it's happening now

00:38:08.157 --> 00:38:10.299
is not vulnerable to the connection.

00:38:10.299 --> 00:38:11.469
- [Ivan] And you could
be sitting in Miami,

00:38:11.469 --> 00:38:12.999
betting on a game in Chicago.

00:38:12.999 --> 00:38:13.832
- That's right.

00:38:13.832 --> 00:38:15.549
Or literally this pitch.

00:38:15.549 --> 00:38:17.819
Because I mean, it really does matter.

00:38:17.819 --> 00:38:20.998
- Yes, now, that may not
be the perfect use of 5G.

00:38:20.998 --> 00:38:23.229
But there may be other things that--

00:38:23.229 --> 00:38:25.019
- Look, somebody getting escapism

00:38:25.019 --> 00:38:26.866
because they love betting
on every pitch of baseball,

00:38:26.866 --> 00:38:29.309
back to, you said
something very important,

00:38:29.309 --> 00:38:31.959
which is the big things
you were doing at Verizon.

00:38:31.959 --> 00:38:34.419
Well for the three-person
startup in Cincinnati

00:38:34.419 --> 00:38:35.619
that you're helping,

00:38:35.619 --> 00:38:37.529
they're problems are the
biggest problems, to them,

00:38:37.529 --> 00:38:38.749
in the world.

00:38:38.749 --> 00:38:43.448
To the person who literally
got shot and is in a hospital,

00:38:43.448 --> 00:38:44.709
and can be helped by somebody

00:38:44.709 --> 00:38:47.129
who understands this situation
better than anybody there,

00:38:47.129 --> 00:38:49.429
and can electronically do
it from somewhere away,

00:38:49.429 --> 00:38:50.262
guess what?

00:38:50.262 --> 00:38:52.019
Staying alive is the
biggest thing in the world.

00:38:52.019 --> 00:38:53.659
But the person sitting
in their living room

00:38:53.659 --> 00:38:56.559
who's not happy with their
life and needs escapism,

00:38:56.559 --> 00:38:58.399
and needs to gamble on a baseball game

00:38:58.399 --> 00:39:00.199
to be able to get through their day,

00:39:00.199 --> 00:39:02.269
that's their most important
thing at their life.

00:39:02.269 --> 00:39:04.040
And I think that's just humans.

00:39:04.040 --> 00:39:07.679
- I'll use my famous whipping toy, here.

00:39:07.679 --> 00:39:09.399
So you think about it,

00:39:09.399 --> 00:39:12.722
I grew up in era where you
had to go to a sports event,

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:18.311
go to the stadium, you
participate in sports.

00:39:18.311 --> 00:39:21.009
Now, it's virtual.

00:39:21.009 --> 00:39:24.679
You can watch any soccer game,
any game, any time you want.

00:39:24.679 --> 00:39:26.569
So, the one industry

00:39:26.569 --> 00:39:29.252
that's never changed
its model, is education.

00:39:30.479 --> 00:39:32.249
It just drives me crazy.

00:39:32.249 --> 00:39:33.739
It absolutely drives me crazy

00:39:33.739 --> 00:39:37.629
that we could be teaching
people so much quicker

00:39:37.629 --> 00:39:39.359
if we applied all this technology

00:39:39.359 --> 00:39:40.452
in a way that allows--
- What about the fact

00:39:40.452 --> 00:39:42.219
that we continue to teach people

00:39:42.219 --> 00:39:43.529
to memorize information

00:39:43.529 --> 00:39:46.299
when information is at your fingertips.

00:39:46.299 --> 00:39:48.279
Let's just, how 'bout the curriculum

00:39:48.279 --> 00:39:49.512
that fills the format?

00:39:50.359 --> 00:39:54.499
You know it's absurd what
we're doing with that.

00:39:54.499 --> 00:39:57.279
- Well your audience understands this, so.

00:39:57.279 --> 00:39:58.880
- But it takes--
- But there's hope.

00:39:58.880 --> 00:39:59.713
There's really a lot of hope.

00:39:59.713 --> 00:40:04.016
- Its also on us, this goes
back to accountability,

00:40:04.016 --> 00:40:07.609
if parents continue to think

00:40:07.609 --> 00:40:11.559
a diploma from a college is the KPI,

00:40:11.559 --> 00:40:15.189
and is going to set their kids
up for happiness and success,

00:40:15.189 --> 00:40:17.489
we will continue to be vulnerable.

00:40:17.489 --> 00:40:19.040
I think, you know it's funny,

00:40:19.040 --> 00:40:20.779
I think that television commercials

00:40:20.779 --> 00:40:23.229
are grossly overpriced media.

00:40:23.229 --> 00:40:26.569
And I've been advocating that
as an adman of the new version

00:40:26.569 --> 00:40:28.059
for the last decade.

00:40:28.059 --> 00:40:30.389
I just thought that the
biggest brands in the world

00:40:30.389 --> 00:40:32.579
would become more educated over time

00:40:32.579 --> 00:40:34.759
and know how to spend
their money more wisely.

00:40:34.759 --> 00:40:37.429
I did not anticipate OTT networks,

00:40:37.429 --> 00:40:39.699
like Hulu, and Amazon Prime, and Netflix,

00:40:39.699 --> 00:40:42.289
to completely eliminate, over time,

00:40:42.289 --> 00:40:45.919
people even consuming the
content of the big three networks

00:40:45.919 --> 00:40:47.909
let alone the commercials.

00:40:47.909 --> 00:40:49.609
I feel like that's what's
going to happen with education.

00:40:49.609 --> 00:40:51.263
This is not gonna be the education system

00:40:51.263 --> 00:40:52.769
figuring itself out,

00:40:52.769 --> 00:40:55.609
because there's too many
inherent interests, needs,

00:40:55.609 --> 00:40:57.509
and putting history on a pedestal.

00:40:57.509 --> 00:40:59.299
This is something coming from the outside,

00:40:59.299 --> 00:41:01.979
disrupting at another level,
that it has no choice.

00:41:01.979 --> 00:41:03.803
- Yeah, well there isn't enough
competition in education.

00:41:03.803 --> 00:41:06.129
- The end. The end.
- That's part of the problem.

00:41:06.129 --> 00:41:08.183
- The cab industry wasn't
gonna make it better for us

00:41:08.183 --> 00:41:11.999
so that we could get a phone
to pick me up in two seconds

00:41:11.999 --> 00:41:13.399
'cause time is valuable to me.

00:41:13.399 --> 00:41:17.039
It took an outside force,
and then everybody kicks in.

00:41:17.039 --> 00:41:22.039
- You know, for many years,
one of the challenges we had

00:41:22.235 --> 00:41:25.369
was kids coming out of
either high school or college

00:41:26.469 --> 00:41:28.029
didn't have the minimum requirements

00:41:28.029 --> 00:41:30.319
to do the jobs that we had.
- I get it.

00:41:30.319 --> 00:41:31.789
- So we would have to spend--

00:41:31.789 --> 00:41:33.546
- [Gary] From an engineering standpoint?

00:41:33.546 --> 00:41:35.322
- Not those jobs, but you know,

00:41:35.322 --> 00:41:38.069
customer facing jobs.
- I see.

00:41:38.069 --> 00:41:39.558
- Technicians jobs.
- I see.

00:41:39.558 --> 00:41:43.439
- We spent a lot of money
training people to do things

00:41:43.439 --> 00:41:45.259
that they should've learned in school.

00:41:45.259 --> 00:41:46.269
- I get it.

00:41:46.269 --> 00:41:48.209
Listen, we could have 17 episodes

00:41:48.209 --> 00:41:51.169
of our points of view on school, clearly.

00:41:51.169 --> 00:41:54.645
- No, I'm positive about it
because I think this will,

00:41:54.645 --> 00:41:57.089
there is a will in this country.

00:41:57.089 --> 00:41:59.519
And as long as we don't
destroy the culture

00:41:59.519 --> 00:42:02.309
of people wanting to succeed

00:42:02.309 --> 00:42:03.861
and live the American Dream, and work.

00:42:03.861 --> 00:42:07.360
This summersault

00:42:07.360 --> 00:42:08.209
kind of thing.
- We're now back

00:42:08.209 --> 00:42:09.189
to not getting political.

00:42:09.189 --> 00:42:10.022
We won't destroy.

00:42:10.022 --> 00:42:12.075
That's the DNA of this place.

00:42:12.075 --> 00:42:14.339
This ebbs and flows, but
the DNA of this place.

00:42:14.339 --> 00:42:17.129
Now, look, every empire
falls for a reason.

00:42:17.129 --> 00:42:18.252
Too much success.

00:42:19.749 --> 00:42:23.503
- Well, there's a lot of
competition in the world.

00:42:23.503 --> 00:42:26.959
So I think, if anything,
we'll have to respond to that.

00:42:26.959 --> 00:42:31.279
- Yeah, the kid growing up
in China as an only child

00:42:31.279 --> 00:42:32.809
in a lower class family,

00:42:32.809 --> 00:42:34.979
is way more hungry than you.

00:42:34.979 --> 00:42:36.369
And that's gonna play out.

00:42:36.369 --> 00:42:37.949
- It will.
- It sure will.

00:42:37.949 --> 00:42:39.055
I appreciate you being on the show.

00:42:39.055 --> 00:42:42.956
- Thank you for having us, thank you.

